  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a proposed way to measure the thorium-229 nuclear transition using a vacuum-ultraviolet (VUV) frequency comb and a crystal containing thorium atoms. A laser beam from the VUV comb passes through a calcium fluoride crystal doped with thorium. If one of the comb frequencies matches the thorium nuclear transition, the thorium atom appears to absorb the light and then emit a photon that is detected.\n\nThe main idea is to search for the unknown thorium transition frequency by scanning or comparing it against the regularly spaced lines of the comb spectrum. On the right, the comb is shown as a series of discrete blue spectral lines, with one marked as corresponding to the thorium-229 nuclear transition. Another marker indicates the frequency of a strontium atomic transition, measured by a strontium clock. This suggests the comb is referenced to the highly accurate strontium clock so the thorium resonance can be determined precisely.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a precision-spectroscopy scheme: use a clock-referenced VUV frequency comb to excite thorium nuclei in a transparent crystal and detect the resonance through emitted photons.",
